{:iteration/id "query-cost-coscientist-01"
 :iteration/date "2026-08-23"
 :iteration/seed "Generate -> Reflect -> Rank (Elo) -> Evolve -> Meta over kotobase's read path, in the shape design-quality.coscientist runs it for UI (root ADR-2606141500, isekai ADR-0007). The judge is scripts/audit_query_cost.cljs in kotoba-lang/kotobase-server: deterministic, no LLM, counts rather than milliseconds."

 :judge
 {:script "kotobase-server scripts/audit_query_cost.cljs"
  :unit :asks
  :why-not-wall-clock "ADR-2608021000 settled this workspace on counts: this workstation runs many concurrent agent sessions and a millisecond here is a number about the host. Asks are a number about the design."
  :floor "Each shape declares :floor-asks -- the index probes an ideal plan needs. For an n-clause conjunction that is n: read each clause's relation once, join in memory. Headroom is measured minus floor."
  :fitness-correction
  "The first fitness was :floor-quads, quads asked for, and it was WRONG. Quads asked is dominated by the per-request scan cache source-for installs, so the recursive rules looked like the whole problem (75 quads asked against a 3-row answer) while the cache was already absorbing them to 2 physical scans. Counting ASKS moved the entire headroom onto the joins, which is where it is. Recorded because a co-scientist loop is exactly as honest as its judge, and this one was briefly measuring the wrong thing."
  :answer-guard "Every shape runs BOTH ways every run and the result SETS are compared. A row COUNT does not say the rows are the same rows, and a path that asks less by answering less is not cheaper. AGREE 9/9."}

 :baseline
 {:graph "963 quads -- a 4-level class chain, 60 individuals, 300 people with :knows/:age/:city"
  :measured
  [{:shape :point                   :rows 1  :asks 1   :floor 1 :headroom 0}
   {:shape :predicate-scan          :rows 60 :asks 1   :floor 1 :headroom 0}
   {:shape :predicate-value         :rows 60 :asks 1   :floor 1 :headroom 0}
   {:shape :two-clause-join         :rows 60 :asks 61  :floor 2 :headroom 59}
   {:shape :three-clause-join       :rows 40 :asks 601 :floor 3 :headroom 598}
   {:shape :subclass-closure        :rows 3  :asks 6   :floor 1 :headroom 5}
   {:shape :type-closure            :rows 4  :asks 6   :floor 2 :headroom 4}
   {:shape :closure-all-individuals :rows 60 :asks 6   :floor 2 :headroom 4}
   {:shape :count-aggregate         :rows 1  :asks 1   :floor 1 :headroom 0}]
  :total-headroom 670
  :finding "98% of the headroom is two shapes. Both are joins, and both are index-nested-loop against a source this server had ALREADY fetched into memory -- N keyed scans of resident data where one broad scan would do."}

 :generate
 [{:id "qc-h1"
   :title "Supply :clause-cardinality from the prefetched source"
   :change "pattern-source/clause-cardinality counts each clause's broad relation against the in-memory source and hands it to datalog.core/q, which then chooses one broad scan plus an in-memory hash join over N keyed scans."
   :grounding "The hash-join path has existed in datalog.core since 2026-08-02 (arrangement 7a0a2002, ADR-2608021000 6-4) and fires ONLY when the caller supplies :clause-cardinality. This server never did. The mechanism was not missing; the hint was."
   :predicted-gain 657
   :effort :S}
  {:id "qc-h2"
   :title "Order clauses by measured selectivity before joining"
   :change "sort :where by counted cardinality ascending so the smallest relation drives"
   :predicted-gain 0
   :why-zero "qc-h1 already reaches the floor on both join shapes -- there is no ask left to remove. This would reduce the BINDINGS carried between clauses, which the current fitness does not measure. Deferred with the reason rather than shipped on a hunch."
   :effort :M}
  {:id "qc-h3"
   :title "Collapse the fixpoint's repeated asks"
   :change "the semi-naive loop re-asks the same pattern every round; hoist the scan out of the round"
   :predicted-gain 13
   :effort :M
   :note "Physically already absorbed: 6 asks collapse to 2 distinct patterns through the per-request cache. The remaining cost is the ask, not the scan."}
  {:id "qc-h4"
   :title "Narrow the prefetch"
   :change "prefetch each clause at its narrowest planned pattern rather than its widest"
   :predicted-gain 0
   :rejected-by-measurement "prefetched = 643 for the three-clause join, which is exactly 300 + 300 + 43, the sum of the three ranges. There is nothing to narrow. Also unsafe: the fixpoint probes with values not known at plan time (the same reason compiled-patterns widens a variable-length path)."
   :effort :M}
  {:id "qc-h5"
   :title "Push the count aggregate down"
   :change "compute COUNT during the join instead of materialising rows"
   :predicted-gain 0
   :rejected-by-measurement "measured 1 ask, at the floor. query-exec already pushes it."
   :effort :S}
  {:id "qc-h6"
   :title "Memoise the source across requests by chain CID"
   :change "a content-addressed source cache, the shape materialize-memo already uses"
   :predicted-gain 0
   :out-of-scope "This judge measures ONE query. A cross-request win needs a different judge and would be measured in block reads, not asks. Named so it is not re-derived."
   :effort :M}
  {:id "qc-h7"
   :title "Use cardinality instead of query for fully-bound clauses"
   :change "datalog.query/cardinality counts without building the set"
   :predicted-gain 0
   :out-of-scope "A memory win, not an ask win. Needs a memory judge."
   :effort :S}]

 :rank
 {:method "Elo round-robin, K=32, base 1200, bout decided by predicted gain then effort -- the same convention design-quality.coscientist uses. The measured fitness is the judge; there is no LLM debate."
  :order ["qc-h1" "qc-h3" "qc-h5" "qc-h7" "qc-h2" "qc-h4" "qc-h6"]
  :note "Three of seven were REJECTED BY MEASUREMENT rather than by opinion (qc-h4, qc-h5, and the ask half of qc-h2). That is the point of having a judge: the loop spent its ranking on the one hypothesis with headroom behind it."}

 :evolve
 {:batch-id "query-cost-kaizen-1"
  :members ["qc-h1"]
  :why-only-one "qc-h3 is the only other non-zero, at 2% of the total and already absorbed physically by the cache. Shipping it in the same pass would put an unmeasurable change next to a measurable one in the same delta."}

 :shipped
 {:hypothesis "qc-h1"
  :where ["kotoba-lang/kotobase-server pattern-source/clause-cardinality"
          "handler/do-q (datomic.q Datalog form)"
          "handler/run-indexed-compiled-graph-query (graph.query)"]
  :not-applied-to "sparql-protocol/do-sparql -- that surface does not hand its algebra to datalog.core at all. It materialises the named ranges into a quad seq and runs sparql.core's own BGP, which joins in memory already, so there is no keyed-scan-per-binding path to replace. Written into the source rather than left as an absence."
  :measured-after
  [{:shape :two-clause-join   :asks-before 61  :asks-after 2 :floor 2}
   {:shape :three-clause-join :asks-before 601 :asks-after 3 :floor 3}]
  :total-headroom-after 13
  :closed "670 -> 13, 98%. Both join shapes land exactly on their floor."
  :answers "AGREE 9/9 -- every shape compared as a SET, both ways, in the same run."
  :discrimination "Making clause-cardinality return nils turns both join tests red. 174 tests / 649 assertions in kotobase-server, with the 7 pre-existing nbb-only failures in hosted-client-transactions-... unchanged."}

 :seed-for-next
 [{:what "The fixpoint's remaining 13 asks (qc-h3)."
   :needs "A judge that separates asks from physical scans, since the cache already absorbs these. The audit reports both columns; nothing acts on the difference yet."}
  {:what "Bindings carried between clauses (qc-h2)."
   :needs "A fitness over intermediate result size. Asks cannot see it -- both join shapes are already at the floor and would score identically before and after."}
  {:what "Cross-request source reuse (qc-h6)."
   :needs "A judge over a REQUEST SEQUENCE, measured in block reads. The one-query judge here is structurally blind to it."}
  {:what "Re-measure LDBC."
   :needs "The interleaved harness in kotobase-peer. ADR-2608021000's ratios (IC09 12.8x, IC08 12.6x) predate this change and predate the index path; nothing here claims to have moved them, and the claim should be measured rather than inferred from the ask counts above."}]}
